# A DECK'S UNDERSIDE — the roof's soffit, one axis over.
#
# ⚠ THE ROOF'S FIX DID NOT REACH A FLOOR, and the EYES work named it as the one thing
# still owed: *"a DECK is a sheet, so EYES under an upper storey looks through it
# exactly as it once looked through a roof."* `emit_floor_slab` has drawn a RIM down
# the side of every deck since the layers were first drawn, so a deck has LOOKED like
# it had a thickness — with no face across the bottom of it.
#
# ⚠ AND A PICTURE CANNOT SEE IT INSIDE A HOUSE. A roof's soffit and a floor's soffit
# are one colour and one surface, so standing under an upper storey photographs
# `soffit 0.997` whether the deck has an underside or you are seeing the ROOF through
# it. Two instruments are needed and this file uses both: the COUNT on the wire, and
# a picture taken where a deck is the only thing overhead.

echo --- ground to stand on, made by levelling as we walk
# ⚠ `rate 0` IS LOAD-BEARING HERE, NOT TIDINESS — plan 22 `T0`. At the default rate
# `step 90` means *wait for 90 more ticks*, and the ticks are paced by the WALL CLOCK,
# so the walk is 90 plus whatever `keys 1`'s sleep and the `4:0` round-trip add.
# Measured, exact stepping, one run per tick count:
#
#     86, 88 ticks -> 3e7ef3b5f47649025b54ccac77244af4
#     90 … 98      -> cea971a07899e420b344c0054567f4e1
#
# **The world is a STEP FUNCTION of the tick count and this script sat one to two
# ticks above the cliff** — reproducible only because every source of jitter ADDS
# ticks, so the run drifted up into a plateau nine ticks wide and never down over the
# edge. At `rate 0` nothing ticks except a `step`, so the walk is exactly 90 by
# construction and the world is the same `cea971a0…` on any box.
#
# ⚠ AND THE CLIFF IS THIS WALK'S LENGTH, NOT LEVELLING'S. `cellar.keys` levels too and
# has margin either way, because its arms are 2.67 wu — inside `LEVEL_R`'s disc, where
# two more ticks reach no new hex. This walk is 9.60 wu, so its END lands on a cell
# boundary and two ticks can cross one.
rate 0
at 0 0 0
verb raise
wait rebuilt
send 6:1
keys 1
step 90
keys 0
send 6:0
step 30

echo --- the soffit BEFORE: no roof anywhere, so there is nothing to have one
mesh soffit 0 0

echo --- a storey over the level ground, with open sky above it
# ⚠ `storey`, NOT `storey_up` — plan 22 `K3`. It is one of a PAIR that stays two verbs
# (a direction belongs in the action), and the downward one is `cellar` rather than
# `storey_down` because the domain has that word. See `cellar.keys`.
verb storey
step 60
# ⚠ 342 IS 19 CELLS x 18 VERTICES — one six-triangle fan per cell of the disc the
# storey wrote, which the ack reports as "storey +1 on 19 cells". A structural
# relation, not a magic number: every deck cell that gets a top gets an underside.
mesh soffit 342 342
mesh floor

echo --- EYES, under the deck, looking STRAIGHT UP
send 40:4
send 3:0,-20000
step 120
snap s16-under-deck
# ⚠ `floor` IS THE ROW, NOT `sky`. Nothing culls backfaces here, so a deck with no
# underside is not a hole — it is its own TOP FAN seen from below, in the floor's
# timber. So the question is which of the two surfaces is overhead, and the pair is
# disjoint: with the underside it is soffit and not floor.
frame 0 1.0 soffit 0.90 1.0 floor 0 0.05 sky 0 0.001
save t1deckfull
